\chapter{Conclusion}
\label{ch:conclusion}

\section{Summary of the Thesis}

This thesis investigated how causal concept faithfulness can be measured when concepts in an input cannot be treated as independent units of evidence. The starting point was the \emph{Causal Concept Faithfulness} metric proposed by Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}, which compares the influence an LLM attributes to concepts in its explanation with causal effects estimated through individual interventions. Atomic interventions can, however, underestimate or misallocate influence when one concept can be reconstructed from another or when concepts affect an answer jointly.

To address this limitation, the thesis introduced \emph{FELICS} (\emph{Faithfulness Estimation Leveraging Interacting Concept Sets}). An oracle partitions concepts into bounded candidate interaction groups, after which every non-empty coalition within each group is evaluated. Shapley values allocate the observed coalition effects to individual concepts, preserving the concept-level comparison between causal and explanation-implied effects. A reference-relative correction addresses systematic departures from additivity on the selected effect scale, while $n_{\max}$ limits the exponential growth in counterfactuals. FELICS was formalized, implemented, and evaluated on BBQ, MedQA, and the newly constructed tabular dataset New German Credit. The oracle acts as a screening mechanism: it proposes relationships worth examining, while counterfactual model responses provide the measured coalition effects.

\section{Answers to the Research Questions}

The evaluation of \rqref{rq:correlation_diff} did not establish a general association between oracle-derived interaction-presumption strength and changes in causal effects or faithfulness. A positive relationship with CE changes emerged in one MedQA condition, but did not generalize. Larger or more numerous candidate groups therefore indicate greater scope for joint evaluation, rather than demonstrating greater response-level interaction.

For \rqref{rq:convergence}, FELICS is theoretically compatible with the original metric when $n_{\max}=1$: under matching inputs and effect estimates, its construction reduces to that of Matton et al.~\cite{mattonWalkTalkMeasuring2024}. The independently executed pipelines did not establish empirical equivalence within the predefined bounds. Although both used removal-based interventions, separate runs still varied through concept decomposition, counterfactual wording, sampled answers, and explanation interpretation. Practical reproducibility consequently remains unresolved and should be tested with fixed intermediate inputs and model outputs.

The results for \rqref{rq:ranking} show that the reference correction is more than a uniform rescaling. It broadly preserved concept rankings in BBQ and MedQA, but produced more extensive reordering in New German Credit. This may reflect the available reference sets, particularly for larger tabular coalitions, although its cause was not isolated. The interpretation of adjusted rankings therefore depends on the normalization context.

For \rqref{rq:faithfulness_increase}, joint interventions did not systematically increase question-level faithfulness. The direction of change varied between dataset--model combinations. FELICS can revise which concepts receive causal credit, but faithfulness increases only when the revised CE vector aligns more closely with the EE vector. Interaction-aware attribution can accordingly reveal greater, lower, or similar explanation--behaviour alignment.

Finally, the conjunctive hypothesis for \rqref{rq:motivating_example} was not supported. Joint attribution increased the effects assigned to eating disorder and refusal of treatment, but decreased the effect of self-perception for both evaluated models. The result nevertheless demonstrated that coalition measurements can materially redistribute causal credit. The unavailable reference correction for the three-concept grand coalition and the small conditional contribution of self-perception are plausible explanations, while also exposing dependencies that future evaluations should control.

\section{Research Contribution}

The principal conceptual contribution of this thesis is to make the correlated-concepts problem an explicit component of behavioural faithfulness measurement. FELICS distinguishes relationships presumed by an oracle from interactions expressed in the evaluated model's answer distribution. Statistical association and semantic similarity can thereby guide the allocation of a limited counterfactual budget without being interpreted as evidence of interaction themselves.

Methodologically, FELICS adds bounded joint interventions and Shapley-based attribution to Causal Concept Faithfulness while retaining its individual concept representation. The empirical examples show the value of this extension. Joint removal can reveal threshold-like behaviour obscured by singleton interventions. It can also partially mitigate an overly fine concept decomposition: if the auxiliary model extracts overlapping descriptions of the same evidence and the oracle groups them, their coalition effect can limit the extent to which they are treated as independent sources of influence. Other cases show that a plausible group may exhibit little consequential interaction, or that a large coalition effect may be confounded by unintended counterfactual edits. FELICS makes these behaviours inspectable, but does not determine their interpretation automatically.

The thesis also contributes a reference implementation for natural-language and tabular inputs, the New German Credit dataset, and corresponding oracle variants. Its evaluation demonstrates local attribution changes that atomic interventions alone cannot reveal, while rejecting the stronger expectation that interaction awareness must produce higher aggregate faithfulness. FELICS preserves concept-level outputs and was computationally feasible at the evaluated values of $n_{\max}$. Its scope remains bounded: singleton interventions are still required for Shapley attribution, interactions across group boundaries remain unmeasured, and normalization makes the final attribution reference-relative. FELICS should therefore be understood as a practical interaction-aware diagnostic framework rather than a procedure for recovering a unique ground-truth interaction structure.

\section{Outlook}

The empirical conclusions are limited by two response models, one auxiliary model, three datasets, and comparatively small response samples. Counterfactual validity was inspected only in selected cases, and no external interaction ground truth was available. New German Credit demonstrates a tabular application but, as a constructed dataset with one repeated decision format, cannot establish generality across tabular tasks.

Further work should validate the oracle and attribution procedure using small concept sets whose complete power sets can be evaluated, complemented by synthetic tasks with known response mechanisms. Comparing the resulting attribution across values of $n_{\max}$ would connect omitted interaction effects with computational cost. Evaluation across additional models and datasets, systematic counterfactual review, structured auxiliary-model outputs, and explicit failure reporting would further improve reproducibility.

The reference correction also requires sensitivity analysis. Reporting raw and adjusted Shapley allocations together would expose its influence, while matched or partially pooled reference estimates may improve stability when few comparable treatments exist. Alternative effect scales could reduce systematic non-additivity and lessen the need for normalization. A graded EE estimator could additionally preserve distinctions between moderate and decisive influence, provided that the scale and auxiliary judgments are validated.

Measuring explanation faithfulness requires attention to how concepts overlap, substitute for one another, and matter in combination. FELICS provides a concrete framework for incorporating these relationships into behavioural faithfulness measurement. Its evaluation shows that joint interventions expose causal attribution patterns that atomic interventions can conceal, while also revealing the dependence of those patterns on concept representation, counterfactual construction, grouping, and normalization. These findings provide a basis for faithfulness measures that represent the structure of model evidence more adequately and communicate their assumptions more transparently.
